Yes, after posting I thought a bit more about that yesterday.

My opinion is that this game has a very powerful lure toward the first  
ascencion. At least for me.
I don't know if it's because I had something to prove to myself or to  
other or what, but the point is that the first ascencion seems very  
important to me. Actually, once I ascended Nethack, I had some feeling of  
pointlessness in playing again and switched to Slash'em to have "something  
new" to do (obviously, staying within Nethack can provide novelty...) And  
once I ascended Slash'em, I patched it to have again "something new". But  
this time, I played Nethack again and such.

Well, to sum up, for me there was clearly a before and an after the first  
ascencion and before I was only motivated by ascending and after I start  
considering the importance of learning the skills to play correctly.

For this reason, I think that the first ascencion is worth using all the  
tricks in the book. For a first ascencion, I will of course not consider  
any conduct as being sensible. Read teh spoilers. Wish for artefacts if  
needed. Farm pudding as much as one want. Even reroll weasling doesn't  
tarnish the accomplishment of a first ascencion in my opinion.
Reroll weasling is not that different from actually playing the "bad" game  
and dying quickly while savescumming uses an out-of-game mechanism (the  
computer) and I consider this one cheating...

Of course, it's far more useful in the long rum to learn how to play with  
Sleep rather than Heal, but in my early game I went much further with Heal  
than Sleep. And I did reroll sometimes. I probably stopped by the time I  
ascended (can't remember) and I don't know which one I had that time.

To some extend, the same thing exists for tricks that have not be done  
yet. I wasn't there at the time of the first pacific ascencion ever, but  
if at that time someone said "I can ascend a pacifist only if I roll a  
wizard with the exact starting gear I need", I would have say go ahead and  
reroll as wanted. Even the bone-stuffed full conduct ascencion that was  
done some time ago, I consider it as a proof of concept and if now somehow  
claim to be able to do the same without bones but with the proper starting  
gear, I won't scorn about reroll weasling.


So, yes, overall I agree that it is better to learn to use what one gets  
rather than rely on some unusual gear to win the game, but I think there  
is an exception for the first time.

